        },
        {
          "id": "p10-kaprekar-introduction",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Choose a set of three digits. Repeats are allowed, but the digits are not ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "all",
              "marks": [
                "italic"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " allowed to be the same (so 176 and 838 are okay but 444 is not). Now carry out the following steps.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p10-kaprekar-steps",
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": true,
          "start": 1,
          "indent": 7,
          "items": [
            {
              "id": "p10-kaprekar-step1",
              "type": "list-item",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "id": "p10-kaprekar-step1-text",
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "fontSize": null,
                  "align": "left",
                  "spaceBefore": 0,
                  "spaceAfter": 2,
                  "lineHeight": 1.4,
                  "indent": 0,
                  "inlines": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Make the largest three-digit number you can out of them.",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p10-kaprekar-step2",
              "type": "list-item",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "id": "p10-kaprekar-step2-text",
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "fontSize": null,
                  "align": "left",
                  "spaceBefore": 0,
                  "spaceAfter": 2,
                  "lineHeight": 1.4,
                  "indent": 0,
                  "inlines": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Then reverse this number to get the smallest number possible. This may involve putting a zero at the front of a number.",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p10-kaprekar-step3",
              "type": "list-item",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "id": "p10-kaprekar-step3-text",
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "fontSize": null,
                  "align": "left",
                  "spaceBefore": 0,
                  "spaceAfter": 2,
                  "lineHeight": 1.4,
                  "indent": 0,
                  "inlines": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Then subtract the smaller number from the larger one. If the result of the subtraction is two digits, then a 0 is placed to the left of the number.",
                      "marks": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p10-kaprekar-example-text",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Now go back to Step 1 with this number and repeat the process until you notice something special happening. For example, if you choose 680, the first step gives 860, step 2 gives 068, step 3 gives ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "860-68=792",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " and then you start again with 792 … ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "972-279=\\ldots",
              "display": false
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p10-kaprekar-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Describe what eventually happens.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Eventually the result is $495$, which then repeats.",
          "worked": "For the supplied starting digits:\n$$\\begin{align*}860-068&=792\\\\972-279&=693\\\\963-369&=594\\\\954-459&=495\\end{align*}$$\nThe digits of $495$ again give $954-459=495$, so every subsequent result is $495$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-kaprekar-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "What do you notice about the numbers produced on the way?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Each result, written with three digits, has $9$ in the middle. The outside digits add to $9$, so the digit sum is $18$. Every result is a multiple of $99$.",
          "worked": "The possible subtraction results are $099$, $198$, $297$, $396$, $495$, $594$, $693$, $792$ and $891$. Each has middle digit $9$ and outside digits summing to $9$.\n\nTo explain this, write the sorted digits as $a\\ge b\\ge c$, with $a>c$:\n$$\\begin{align*}(100a+10b+c)-(100c+10b+a)&=99(a-c).\\end{align*}$$\nSince $a-c$ is one of $1,2,\\ldots,9$, these are exactly the possible results.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-kaprekar-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p10-kaprekar-definition-text",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "We call the number at which you finally arrive a ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "self-producing integer",
                  "marks": [
                    "bold"
                  ],
                  "colour": "#ef6068"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ". With some sets of three digits, you get it straight away. Other sets take a few iterations but never more than six.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p10-kaprekar-four-digit-transition",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "If the set you start with contains ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "four",
                  "marks": [
                    "italic"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " digits instead of three, you get a different self-producing integer.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p10-kaprekar-c-task",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Find what it is.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$6174$.",
          "worked": "For example, start with $1234$:\n$$\\begin{align*}4321-1234&=3087\\\\8730-0378&=8352\\\\8532-2358&=6174\\\\7641-1467&=6174.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $6174$ is self-producing.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-kaprekar-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p10-kaprekar-d-task",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Find out what happens if you start with a set of ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "five",
                  "marks": [
                    "italic"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " digits. (Don’t be in too much of a hurry to report on your findings here, because there is a bit more to it than meets the eye. It might be useful to have a partner to help you. Also, if you have been doing all your subtractions without the aid of a calculator up until now, well, now might be a good time to get one out!)",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "There is no five-digit self-producing integer. The routine eventually enters one of three cycles:\n\n$63954\\to61974\\to82962\\to75933\\to63954$;\n\n$62964\\to71973\\to83952\\to74943\\to62964$;\n\n$59994\\to53955\\to59994$.",
          "worked": "Continuing the routine reveals two cycles of length four and one of length two:\n$$\\begin{align*}96543-34569&=61974\\\\97641-14679&=82962\\\\98622-22689&=75933\\\\97533-33579&=63954\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}96642-24669&=71973\\\\97731-13779&=83952\\\\98532-23589&=74943\\\\97443-34479&=62964\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}99954-45999&=53955\\\\95553-35559&=59994.\\end{align*}$$\nNone is a fixed point: the results keep cycling. Checking all five-digit digit multisets, excluding identical-digit starts and retaining leading zeros, gives these three eventual cycles.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 112
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-kaprekar-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p10-kaprekar-e-task",
              "type": "paragraph",
